<h2>Can Apple Watch measure cortisol?</h2>
<p><strong>No. Apple Watch does not directly measure cortisol.</strong> It records signals such as heart rate, heart rate variability and sleep that apps can use to describe stress and recovery patterns. A wearable wellness score is different from a laboratory cortisol result.</p>
<p>If you searched for “Apple Watch cortisol” or a cortisol tracker app, this distinction matters: Cortisol+ helps you explore wearable trends, but cannot tell you how much cortisol is in your blood, saliva or urine.</p>
<h2>What the Watch records, and what it cannot establish</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Signal</th><th scope="col">What is recorded</th><th scope="col">Possible use</th><th scope="col">Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Heart rate</td><td>Beats per minute; background samples and workout measurements</td><td>See activity and resting patterns</td><td>A faster pulse has many causes</td></tr>
<tr><td>HRV</td><td>SDNN, a measure of variation in beat intervals</td><td>Compare readings with your own history</td><td>Not a cortisol concentration or diagnosis</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sleep</td><td>Estimated time asleep and supported sleep stages</td><td>Review sleep alongside daytime wellbeing</td><td>Does not establish why you slept poorly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wrist temperature</td><td>Overnight changes on supported models</td><td>Additional context for changes from baseline</td><td>Availability depends on model and settings; not a cortisol sensor</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Apple explains <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/120277">heart rate and HRV measurement</a>, <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108906">sleep tracking</a> and <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102674">nightly wrist temperature</a>. Follow the instructions for your particular Watch and software version.</p>
<h2>Does Apple Watch have a stress score?</h2>
<p>Apple's Mindfulness features support breathing, reflection and recording your State of Mind. A mood check-in is your own report, while a third-party stress score is an interpretation of sensor data. These describe different things and may not agree.</p>
<p>Cortisol+ combines available signals into its own wellness score. Other apps use different inputs and formulas, so a 60 in one app should not be assumed to mean the same thing as a 60 in another.</p>
<h2>How to explore your readings with Cortisol+</h2>
<ol><li>Download Cortisol+ on a compatible iPhone and connect your Apple Watch through Apple Health.</li><li>Choose the Health permissions you want to grant. Missing permissions or limited wear time can reduce available data.</li><li>Open the dashboard and examine the factors behind the score. Look at the HRV, resting heart rate and sleep records, not only the summary number.</li><li>Compare readings over time with notes about sleep, activity and how you feel.</li></ol>
