<p>Some labels in this older development build describe a person's recovery too confidently. Interpret them as software-generated labels, not established facts about your body. Our <a href="/how-it-works/">current methodology and limitations</a> explain that distinction.</p>
<h2>Why readings can be missing or change unexpectedly</h2>
<p>If your latest sample is missing, follow our <a href="/blog/apple-watch-hrv-not-updating/">Apple Watch HRV troubleshooting guide</a> to compare Apple Health timestamps, check app permissions and review watch settings.</p>
<p>Background sampling is intermittent, and data must sync to Apple Health before the app can use it. Movement, fit, charging, permissions and software settings can affect recording. Exercise, illness, alcohol, medication, breathing and time of day can also influence HRV and heart rate. These are reasons to check context, not assume every change represents psychological stress.</p>
<h2>Wearable stress versus cortisol testing</h2>
<p><a href="https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/cortisol-test/">Clinical cortisol tests use blood, saliva or urine</a>, with timing and interpretation matched to the question a clinician is investigating. HRV cannot confirm or rule out a hormone disorder. Seek care for persistent or concerning symptoms rather than using a Watch score to decide whether a hormone test is necessary.</p>
